**Definition 10.1 (additional source-bound actuator).** Suppose an actual
service executes (AC.28) or its mirror on the same source at its unknown
actual height, using the calibrated canonical instrument. Its source-bound
configuration/coupling, waveform implementation, timing and permissions,
blanks and complete event recorder are additional resources. The service
may depend on the actual source through that coupling; it is not assumed
to be a common channel on $V$ and history-independent blanks. Neither
naming the relative path nor knowing (AC.29) acquires this service.

For clarity its complete two-unit operation is specified, including
failures. For either actual waveform let

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda(t)&=(k'(t))_+/k(t)+(-k'(t))_+/(c-k(t)),\\
S(t)&=\exp(-\int_0^t\lambda),\qquad
K_t=\sqrt{S(t)}\operatorname{diag}_{i=0}^4
 \sqrt{p_i(k(t))/p_i(x)},\\
\gamma_i(t)&=\lambda(t)-(\log p_i(k(t)))',\qquad
J_i(t)=\sqrt{\gamma_i(t)}|f\rangle\langle i|K_t.
\end{aligned}
\tag{AC.35}
$$

The live branch is $K_2\rho K_2^*$; failure in a Borel set $A$ and mode
$i$ is $\int_A J_i(t)\rho J_i(t)^*dt$, tensor identity on the actual
reference/archive. Nonnegative rates and
$\sum_i\int_0^2J_i^*J_i=I-K_2^*K_2$ prove completeness, as in OC.19b.
There are no failure atoms at the turn. For the positive loop first-leg
possible failure modes are $1,3,2$; second-leg new failures are $0,13,2$.
Reflection swaps these patterns. The second leg runs only after first-leg
survival. Old failures keep their earlier event and $f$.

